  "sections": {
    "work": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The exaggerated and unnatural musculature of this figure may suggest that the Netherlandish artist Hendrick Goltzius had never studied a real human body. Indeed, he pulled from a mixture of sources including classical sculpture, Italian art, and his imagination.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Cleveland Museum of Art",
          "title": "The Great Hercules or 'Knollenman'",
          "publisher": "Cleveland Museum of Art",
          "readAt": "https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/159194",
          "accessed": "2026-11-03",
          "tier": 4,
          "license": "CC0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "moment": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Goltzius ushered in Mannerism in Northern Europe, a style that featured exaggerated forms and metaphorical and allegorical subject matter.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Cleveland Museum of Art",
          "title": "The Great Hercules or 'Knollenman'",
          "publisher": "Cleveland Museum of Art",
          "readAt": "https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/159194",
          "accessed": "2026-11-03",
          "tier": 4,
          "license": "CC0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Their pictorial language was characterised by a strong awareness of style and cultivated elegance. They strove for artful ingenuity rather than naturalism.",
        "lead": "Of the Haarlem Mannerists:",
